For the TestMain, I follow the pattern from difftest_test.go:\n1. Create a temp dir for binaries\n2. Build Go cat binary\n3. Find gcat\n4. Set package-level vars\n5. Run tests\n6. Cleanup\n\nFor file-based tests, I'll create helper functions to set up files. Each test function will create its own temp dir with the needed files.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is to have separate test functions for each logical group (core I/O vs error handling), and use table-driven DiffTest slices within each.\n\nLooking at the task scope more carefully, I need to focus on R1.1, R1.2, R5.2, and R5.3 only—no tests for line numbering, squeeze, or non-printing display features since those are out of scope.\n\nFor R1.1, I'll test single file reads and multi-file concatenation in order. For R1.2, I need to verify stdin reading with no arguments and with the \"-\" flag. For error handling, I'll test that nonexistent files produce proper stderr messages and that exit codes are 0 on success and 1 on failure.\n\nThe tricky part is that the error messages differ between gcat and our Go implementation—gcat prefixes with \"gcat:\" while ours uses \"cat:\"—so I'll need a normalizer to strip those prefixes for comparison. Writing test structure...\n\nNow I'm thinking through how the reference binary runs first, then my Go implementation, both reading from the same directory—so file order consistency shouldn't be an issue. For the mixed valid/invalid file test, I need to ensure the real file exists in the work directory while referencing a nonexistent one. I'm also considering how to handle the interspersed stdin case where arguments like `[\"file1.txt\", \"-\", \"file2.txt\"]` should read from both files and stdin in sequence.\n\nSince RunDiffTests creates a fresh bytes.NewReader for each binary invocation, both the reference and Go binaries receive identical stdin, which means the interspersed stdin test should work correctly—each binary will read file1, then stdin, then file2 in the same order.\n\nNow I'm structuring the test suite into three focused test functions: one for basic file reading, one for stdin handling including the \"-\" cases, and one for error scenarios and exit codes, each using t.Parallel() for efficiency.\n\nI need to be careful about how the reference implementation handles error messages—specifically whether it uses the program name from argv[0] or just the basename, which will affect how I normalize stderr in the tests. I should be more careful about where the replacement happens—\"gcat:\" could theoretically appear elsewhere in the output, so I'll use a simple bytes replacement that targets \"gcat: \" specifically, which should be safe enough for error messages. Now I'm writing out the test file with the copyright header and differential test setup.\n\nSetting up test infrastructure... I'm finishing the build command execution and checking for errors, then running the test suite and cleaning up the temporary directory before exiting. I'm realizing I need to figure out where the module root is since the test runs from `cmd/cat/` but the build command needs to execute from the project root. Setting cmd.Dir explicitly isn't necessary since the test already runs in the cmd/cat directory, so I can just use `go build -o goBinary .` to build the current package directly.\n\nNow I'm setting up the TestMain function to look for the reference gcat binary on PATH, create a temporary directory for the test binary, and handle any errors that come up...\n\nBuilding the test binary...\n\nThen I'll run the actual tests and clean up the temporary directory afterward. Now I need to write the actual test functions themselves. I'm reconsidering the approach—I could use a package-level flag that each test checks and skips if needed, which aligns better with D2's requirement for t.Skip, even though it's more verbose than just calling os.Exit(0) from TestMain.\n\nSetting up the flag pattern... I'll print a message to stderr and exit cleanly if gcat isn't found, which is the standard Go approach and will show up when tests run with verbose output. Now I'm setting up helper functions for the file-based tests, starting with a utility to write test files and handle errors, then moving on to create a normalizer for stderr output.\n\nDesigning the test cases now...\n\nI need to verify that t.Parallel() is safe here. Since each test creates its own temporary directory and the binary paths are read-only after TestMain, there's no shared mutable state between tests. The RunDiffTests function doesn't call t.Parallel() on its subtests, so calling it on the parent test should be fine.\n\nFor the stdin-interspersed test case, when the arguments include a dash (like [\"file1.txt\", \"-\", \"file2.txt\"]), both binaries should read file1, then the provided stdin bytes, then file2, producing identical output.\n\nI also need to handle the case where tests run from a different directory—since I'm setting the working directory to the temp folder where the test files are created, the relative paths in the arguments will resolve correctly.\n\nWriting the differential test code... Setting up the reference binary path, then creating a temporary directory for the Go binary build. I'm compiling the cat binary with the go build command, handling any build errors, and finally running the test suite before cleaning up the temporary directory.\n\nNow I'm defining helper functions for the tests: one to write test files to disk, another to normalize program names in output so I can compare the GNU reference binary's stderr with the Go implementation's, and starting to work through the actual test cases. Writing test cases... Writing test cases...\n\nI'm verifying the exit codes match the expected behavior—when cat encounters a nonexistent file it exits with code 1, and when processing both a missing and valid file, it still exits 1 while outputting only the valid file's content to stdout and the error to stderr. The Normalize field applies the normalizer functions to both the reference and generated output for comparison. I'm applying normalizers to both stdout and stderr from the reference and Go implementations, with my normalizeProgramName function swapping \"gcat: \" for \"cat: \" — it's a no-op on stdout since that doesn't contain the prefix. Now I'm checking off the acceptance criteria: the test file compiles and passes go vet, all differential tests pass when the gcat binary is available, I've covered single-file reads, multi-file concatenation, stdin-only cases, and stdin with dash arguments interspersed with named files, and I'm handling error cases for nonexistent files with proper exit codes and mixed valid/invalid file scenarios. I still need to verify the copyright header and SPDX license identifier follow project conventions. I'm verifying that the subprocess will correctly find the go.mod file when running `go build .` from the cmd/cat/ directory—Go walks up the directory tree to locate it, so this should work fine. I should also make sure to check for build errors in TestMain before proceeding, and I'll follow the project's convention of using `go build -o bin/<name> ./cmd/<name>/` for the output path.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the test cases from test-rel01.1 that I need to cover: single file passthrough, multiple files concatenated, reading from stdin with \"-\", and handling missing files alongside real ones. I've implemented all of these scenarios plus a few additional cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5555,"cache_read_input_tokens":51534,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5555},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"286c6e79-e6d7-46b2-94a8-acb16c22699c","uuid":"dc5938f5-c0ba-4633-9db2-af5620fe9c54"}
